 "intro_html": "The everyday daifuku of Japanese sweet shops: a big ball of <a href=\"anko.html\">anko</a> in a thin white mochi shell, with nearly as much filling as mochi. The mochi is shiratamako, sugar and water, cooked in the microwave in three short bursts. Chunky tsubuan or smooth koshian both work. Make them the day you eat them, because the mochi goes hard in the fridge. <a href=\"strawberry-daifuku.html\">Strawberry</a>, <a href=\"mame-daifuku.html\">salted pea</a> and <a href=\"yomogi-daifuku.html\">mugwort</a> daifuku follow the same method.",
